  {
    "id": "10.64898/2026.10.25.761677",
    "title": "DNA-PK enforces the S/G2 checkpoint in ATM-deficient cells to limit ATR inhibitor efficacy",
    "authors": [
      "Branigan, T. B.",
      "Alharthi, R.",
      "Kochupurakkal, B. S.",
      "Hao, J.",
      "Revia, S.",
      "Albers, J.",
      "Cai, M.-Y.",
      "Jiao, Y.",
      "McQueen, K.",
      "Mukkavalli, S.",
      "Roundtree, I. A.",
      "Cote, G. M.",
      "Gokhale, P. C.",
      "Parmar, K.",
      "D'Andrea, A. D.",
      "Zimmermann, A.",
      "Shapiro, G. I."
    ],
    "summary": "ATR inhibition has been proposed as a strategy for tumors under replication stress or those harboring ATM deficiency. However, anti-tumor activity is limited by DNA-PK and ATM compensation. Here, we have delineated distinct roles for DNA-PK and ATM during ATR inhibitor exposure. Both kinases are activated in a cell cycle-dependent manner at the S/G2 boundary following ATR inhibition. DNA-PK imposes the S/G2 checkpoint and promotes completion of DNA replication, whereas ATM detects persistent aberrant DNA replication structures with associated DNA damage in late S and G2/M to establish a G2/M checkpoint promoting DNA repair. In ATM-deficient cells and patient tumors, DNA-PK is activated following ATR inhibition and assumes greater versatility by not only enforcing the S/G2 checkpoint but also by detecting aberrant replication structures in S phase in place of ATM, allowing cells to tolerate ATR inhibition. Consequently, DNA-PK inhibition synergizes with ATR inhibition in ATM-deficient cells and enhances ATR inhibitor efficacy in ATM-deficient PDX models. These data support clinical evaluation of combined ATR and DNA-PK inhibition in ATM-deficient cancers.",
    "published": "2026-10-28T00:00:00+00:00",
    "updated": null,
